# The GTM container export is a restore point, not source: it must survive a
# checkout byte-for-byte identical to the file Google Tag Manager served.
# core.autocrlf is true on Windows here, which would otherwise rewrite its LF
# line endings on checkout and break that guarantee.
analytics/gtm-container-export.json -text

# Audit records are evidence: a PDF must survive a checkout byte-for-byte or
# it will not open. core.autocrlf is true here, and git flagged this PDF as
# text on commit, which means a fresh checkout would rewrite its line endings
# and corrupt the file.
*.pdf -text -diff

# WPCode / Thrive mirrors are paste-back restore points, and the backup law
# proves them exact by character count (CLAUDE.md rule 3). core.autocrlf is
# true on Windows here, so a checkout would rewrite their LF endings to CRLF
# and silently change every count — the working copy would no longer be the
# file that was verified against live.
live/wpcode/** -text
live/thrive/** -text

# Exported diffs are applied with `git apply`, which is strict about line
# endings: a patch generated with LF and checked out as CRLF fails to apply
# against the LF source it was made from. core.autocrlf is true here, so
# without this the patch silently stops working on a fresh clone.
*.patch -text
